25NOV2024 @hanoihantrakul
The FrozenUMM-Mel-Decoder tries to generate a mel spectrogram
based on the Pre-VQ Latents of a pre-trained tokenizer.

In all these experiments I used the best performing ConformerUMM_2255_2375_TTS_ROPE
as the tokenizer.

Some special considerations:
- The tokenizer was trained on audio at 24khz and also mono audio. This means
I have added logic to handle resampling 44.1khz audio to be tokenized at 24khz
and then reconstructed at 44.1khz. The pipeline also supports training, tokenizing
and reconstruction at 24khz.
- The goal is to create stereo mel spectrograms. So I have also added logic
to convert a stereo signal into a mono signal so the tokenizer can operate 
on this signal. Then the mel decoder must decode a stereo mel spectrogram
from this token sequence.
- There are different configurations of num_mel_bins. I have defaulted to mel-160
because this was the setting used to train the original ConvUMM-GAN Tokenizer. It 
made it easier for me to bring over the adversarial losses from ConvUMM-GAN to train
the FrozenUMM-Mel-Decoder. mel-128 and mel-100 is also possible. 
- Griffin Lim reconstruction can be applied to any combination of mel bins. However
vocos vocoder specficially requires mel-100 and Ren Yi's pretrained vocoder specifically requires
mel-160. The tokenizer internally uses mel-128 (but this is abstracted away and does not 
affect FrozenUMM-Mel-Decoder implementation)
- The furthest I got was implementing a stereo mag-only representation. I never got
round to implementing a complex stereo representation like Ju-Chiang's work.